    deeperDive: [
      { q: 'Why is this called "anyonyāśraya" (mutual dependence)?', a: 'Anyonyāśraya is a formal logical concept in Indian philosophy: A depends on B for its definition, and B depends on A. This circular dependence means neither A nor B can be established first or independently. It\'s the logical structure that proves emptiness — if everything depends on something else, nothing has inherent self-standing existence.', realLifeExample: 'A "parent" only exists because of a "child," and a "child" only exists because of a "parent." Neither role makes sense without the other. Remove either — both labels collapse.' },
      { q: 'How does complementarity illustrate this?', a: 'Bohr showed that a quantum system\'s wave and particle properties are complementary — you can measure one or the other, but not both simultaneously, and both are needed for a complete description. The system (object) and its measured properties (characteristics) define each other: what you measure determines what the system "is," and the system determines what can be measured.', realLifeExample: 'It\'s like asking "Is light a wave or a particle?" The answer depends on how you look at it. The experiment (measurement) and the result (property) define each other.' },
      { q: 'Is this just epistemological (about knowing) or ontological (about being)?', a: 'Both. Siderits/Katsura emphasise the epistemological reading: we can\'t KNOW or POSIT either without the other. Garfield adds the ontological implication: if neither can be established independently even in principle, then neither has independent EXISTENCE. Mutual dependence in knowledge implies mutual dependence in being.', deeper: 'Candrakīrti: the inability to establish either term independently is not just a limitation of our knowledge but reflects the genuine ontological status of these phenomena — they exist only dependently.' },
      { q: 'Does this apply to scientific categories too?', a: 'Yes. In science, "electron" and "charge" are mutually defining. An electron IS (partly) defined by having charge -1. And charge -1 IS (partly) defined as what electrons have. Scientific categories are just as subject to mutual dependence as philosophical ones. No scientific entity is defined independently of its properties.', realLifeExample: 'What is "gravity" without mass? What is "mass" without gravitational effects? They define each other — Newton\'s law of gravitation PRESUPPOSES both concepts simultaneously.' },
      { q: 'How does this verse move the argument forward from vv.1-3?', a: 'Vv.1-3 showed: entities can\'t precede characteristics (v.1), no bare entities exist (v.2), and characteristics can\'t arise independently (v.3). V.4 draws the explicit conclusion: entity and characteristic are MUTUALLY dependent — each requires the other. This sets up v.5\'s final conclusion: neither has inherent existence.', realLifeExample: 'Steps 1-3: you can\'t have bread without flour, flour doesn\'t exist alone, flour can\'t appear from nowhere. Step 4 (here): bread and flour define each other — one implies the other. Step 5 (next): neither bread nor flour is independently fundamental.' },
      { q: 'Is mutual dependence the same as emptiness?', a: 'Yes — mutual dependence (anyonyāśraya) is one of the key logical structures that demonstrates emptiness (śūnyatā). If A depends on B and B depends on A, then neither A nor B has svabhāva (inherent existence). What lacks inherent existence is empty. Mutual dependence IS emptiness, seen through the lens of logical analysis.', realLifeExample: 'A lock and a key are mutually dependent (the lock is defined by its key, the key by its lock). Neither is independently "lockness" or "keyness" — their identity depends on each other. That dependency IS their emptiness.' },
    ],
