    deeperDive: [
      { q: 'Why is this verse a "meta-level" move?', a: 'Vv.1-5 analyzed specific elements (space, objects, characteristics) and found them empty. V.6 now turns the analysis on the very CONCEPTS OF EXISTENCE AND NONEXISTENCE themselves. It\'s not just "space is empty" but "the categories we used to analyze space are also empty." The tool of analysis is itself subject to the same analysis.', realLifeExample: 'A ruler measures objects. But who measures the ruler? V.6 is like turning the ruler on itself — showing that even our measuring tools (existence/nonexistence) are not absolute.' },
      { q: 'How does quantum superposition undermine the existence/nonexistence binary?', a: 'A quantum system in superposition is in a state that can\'t be described as simply "the particle IS at position X" or "the particle ISN\'T at position X." Both statements are inadequate. The system is in a state that transcends the classical binary. Only measurement forces a definite outcome. This parallels Nāgārjuna\'s point: existence and nonexistence aren\'t absolute categories that independently apply to reality.', realLifeExample: 'Before opening the box, Schrödinger\'s cat isn\'t alive or dead — it\'s in a state that transcends the binary. Our everyday categories ("alive"/"dead") only work after we look.' },
      { q: 'What is the "who knows?" question pointing to?', a: '"Who knows existence and nonexistence?" challenges the knower — the consciousness that applies these categories. If both categories are mutually dependent, then the knower who uses them is also caught in the web of dependence. There\'s no independent "view from nowhere" from which to absolutely judge "exists" vs. "doesn\'t exist." Even the observer is part of the dependent system.', realLifeExample: 'A judge in a courtroom relies on laws. But who judges the laws? And who judges the judges? There\'s no position completely outside the system.' },
      { q: 'Does this lead to relativism?', a: 'No. Mutual dependence of categories is not relativism (anything goes). Conventionally, existence and nonexistence are perfectly valid and useful. We can say "the cup exists" with full confidence. What Nāgārjuna denies is that these categories are ABSOLUTE, INHERENT features of reality itself. They\'re frameworks we apply, not ultimate truths about the structure of being.', realLifeExample: '"Left" and "right" are mutually dependent — but that doesn\'t mean you can\'t give driving directions. The categories work perfectly; they\'re just not absolute features of space itself.' },
      { q: 'How does non-Boolean quantum logic relate here?', a: 'Classical logic is Boolean: every proposition is either true or false (law of excluded middle). Quantum logic replaces this with a non-Boolean lattice where propositions can be in superposition — neither simply true nor simply false. This formal mathematical framework shows that the binary of existence/nonexistence isn\'t logically necessary — alternative frameworks exist. Nāgārjuna\'s point is similar: the binary is a conventional framework, not an absolute logical necessity.', deeper: 'Birkhoff and von Neumann (1936) showed quantum mechanics requires a non-distributive lattice of propositions — formally breaking classical Boolean logic. This is the mathematical counterpart of Nāgārjuna\'s philosophical argument.' },
      { q: 'How does v.6 connect to the concluding vv.7-8?', a: 'V.6 extends the emptiness analysis from elements (vv.1-5) to the very categories of existence/nonexistence. V.7 will apply this conclusion to space specifically (space is neither entity nor nonentity) and generalise to all elements. V.8 will critique those who cling to existence/nonexistence as absolute categories ("fools and reificationists"). The progression: empty elements → empty categories → peace through letting go.', realLifeExample: 'First you learn a specific lesson (vv.1-5). Then you realise the lesson applies to the framework itself (v.6). Then you apply it universally (v.7). Then you let go of all frameworks and find peace (v.8).' },
    ],
