    title: 'Space Transcends All Four Categories',
    sanskrit: { text: 'tasmān nākāśam asti na bhāvo nābhāvas tathā | na lakṣaṇaṃ na lakṣyaṃ ca ṣaḍ dhātavaḥ samā ākāśavat ||', transliteration: 'tasmān nākāśam asti na bhāvo nābhāvas tathā | na lakṣaṇaṃ na lakṣyaṃ ca ṣaḍ dhātavaḥ samā ākāśavat', translation: 'Therefore, space is not an entity. It is not a nonentity. Not characterised, not without character. The same is true of the other five elements.' },
    philosophy: {
      insight: 'Space transcends all four categories: (1) not an inherent entity, (2) not a nonentity, (3) not inherently characterised, (4) not without character. This fourfold negation (catuṣkoṭi) applies equally to ALL six elements: earth, water, fire, air, space, and consciousness. The analysis generalises: everything that exists is beyond these fixed conceptual categories.',
      madhyamaka: 'Garfield: this applies the catuṣkoṭi (tetralemma) to space and then generalises. The four negations deny: (1) inherent existence (bhāva), (2) inherent nonexistence (abhāva), (3) inherent characterisation (lakṣaṇa), (4) inherent lack of characterisation (alakṣaṇa). By extending to the "other five elements," Nāgārjuna shows this isn\'t a special feature of space but the universal condition of all elements. Siderits/Katsura: the generalisation is crucial — it means the ENTIRE elemental basis of Buddhist cosmology is empty. All six dhātus lack inherent existence. Candrakīrti: this is the Middle Way — rejecting both extremes (entity/nonentity) and both characterisation options.',
      quantum: 'Quantum Vacuum — Neither Something Nor Nothing: The quantum vacuum is the ground state of quantum fields. It\'s not "empty space" (it buzzes with virtual particle-antiparticle pairs). But it\'s not a classical "thing" either (it has no definite particle content). It\'s not characterised by definite properties (virtual particles are fleeting). But it\'s not without character (it has measurable effects like the Casimir effect and Lamb shift). The quantum vacuum transcends all four classical categories — just like Nāgārjuna\'s space.',
      bridge: 'Both frameworks describe a reality that transcends the fourfold classification: (1) not a thing, (2) not nothing, (3) not definitively characterised, (4) not without character. The quantum vacuum and Madhyamaka space both occupy this "middle" position beyond fixed categories.',
      accessible: 'Space isn\'t just "nothing" (it has properties like expansion, dimension, curvature). But it\'s not a "thing" either (you can\'t grab it). It has characteristics (extension, capacity) but isn\'t defined by fixed characteristics (it can curve, expand, vibrate). The quantum vacuum is the same: not empty, not full, not fixed, not featureless. Beyond all four boxes.',
      twoTruths: 'Conventionally, we call space a "thing" with "properties" and use it productively in physics. Ultimately, space doesn\'t fit any fixed category — entity, nonentity, characterised, or uncharacterised. The conventional labels work; the ultimate reality transcends them.',
      commonMisconception: 'NOT saying space is mysterious or unknowable. Saying our conceptual categories (entity/nonentity, characterised/uncharacterised) are too rigid to capture what space actually is. Space is perfectly knowable — just not through these fixed boxes.',
    },
